  completeExplanation: 'Curiosity Chapter 7 opens with the insightful quote by renowned Indian scientist Anna Mani: "Wrong measurements are worse than no measurements at all." In Shillong, Phiban touches her brother Lambok\'s forehead and suspects a fever, but verifies with a clinical thermometer that his body temperature is completely normal. In Activity 7.1, students place one hand in warm water and the other in ice-cold water, then plunge both into tap water. The hand from warm water senses the tap water as cool, while the hand from ice water senses it as warm. This proves that human skin senses the rate and direction of heat flow rather than true temperature. Temperature is scientifically defined as the reliable quantitative measure of the hotness or coldness of a body. Thermometers are categorized into clinical and laboratory instruments. Digital clinical thermometers utilize electronic thermistors/heat sensors to measure body temperature without toxic mercury. Non-contact infrared thermometers detect thermal radiation from forehead skin, playing a vital role in infection control during COVID-19. Human body temperature ranges between 35  degC and 42  degC, with a healthy adult average of 37.0  degC (98.6  degF). Armpit measurements read 0.5  degC to 1.0  degC lower. Three temperature scales are used: Celsius ( degC), Fahrenheit ( degF), and Kelvin (K). The SI base unit of temperature is kelvin (K), converted via T(K) = T( degC) + 273.15. Kelvin symbols omit the degree sign (e.g. 300 K). Laboratory thermometers have a broader range (-10  degC to 110  degC) with alcohol (red) or mercury in a uniform glass stem. Least count is found by dividing the interval between numbered marks by the number of sub-divisions (e.g. 10  degC / 10 = 1  degC). When measuring liquids, the bulb must remain submerged without touching beaker walls, held vertically, and read while submerged (since lacking a constriction causes the liquid thread to recede upon exposure to room air). Phiban\'s class in Shillong observed water boiling at ~98  degC due to reduced atmospheric pressure at high altitude. During boiling and melting, temperature remains constant. The chapter honors Anna Mani (1918-2001), "Weather Woman of India", who built India\'s indigenous meteorological apparatus, and concludes with cosmic temperature extremes: the Sun\'s core at ~15 million  degC and Absolute Zero at 0 K (-273.15  degC).',
